# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: clal-save-aldo-configuration-undocumented-file — saving aldo.conf from AutoLISP drops the self-documentation the debugger writes
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-clal-save-aldo-conf-documentation

* Symptom

Two commands write the *same* file, =$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/clautolisp/aldo.conf=,
and they no longer write it the same way:

- =,settings save= at =DBG>= writes the self-documenting form (1.8.30): the
  settings you changed, then every untouched setting commented out at its
  built-in default with its accepted values;
- =(clal-save-aldo-configuration)= from AutoLISP writes a bare sexp with no
  header and no commented defaults.

So saving from AutoLISP *silently strips* the documentation from a file the
user may have been editing by hand. Nothing breaks — a stripped file reads
back identically — but the next hand edit is done blind.

* Repro (clautolisp 1.8.30)

: clautolisp-sbcl -e '(clal-save-aldo-configuration)'
: head -3 ~/.config/clautolisp/aldo.conf

No =;;;;= header; compare with the file left by =,settings save=.

* Cause

They are two different serialisers over two different stores:

- =clautolisp.debug.ui:WRITE-CONFIGURATION-FILE=
  (=clautolisp/autolisp-debug-ui/source/settings.lisp=) writes the settings
  store, and has =*DEFAULT-ALDO-CONFIGURATION*= / =*SETTING-SPECS*= to
  document it from;
- =SAVE-ALDO-CONFIGURATION-TO= (=clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/source/
  api.lisp=) writes whatever AutoLISP value is bound to
  =*clal-aldo-configuration*=, through the AutoLISP printer.

The obvious fix — call the first from the second — is a *layering violation*:
=clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core= does not depend on
=clautolisp/autolisp-debug-ui= (the dependency runs the other way, via
=config-bridge=), and it should not start to.

There is also a genuine semantic gap, not just a plumbing one:
=*clal-aldo-configuration*= is a user-controlled AutoLISP value, not the
settings store. The user may have put arbitrary keys in it, so "the settings
you did not set" is not well defined on that side the way it is on the
debugger's.

* Options

1. Move the writer down. Put the serialiser plus the defaults/specs tables in
   a layer both can reach (=autolisp-runtime= or a small new system), and have
   both callers use it. Cleanest, most work; the tables' natural home is
   arguably not the UI layer anyway.
2. Have the builtin delegate through the existing bridge. =config-bridge=
   already carries values across the boundary in the other direction; a hook
   variable set by the UI layer at start-up (the pattern
   =*default-on-quit-policy*= uses for sedit) would let the builtin use the
   rich writer when the debugger UI is loaded and fall back to the bare sexp
   when it is not. Smallest change; leaves two code paths.
3. WONTFIX, documented. State in the manual that
   =(clal-save-aldo-configuration)= writes the plain form. Defensible: the
   AutoLISP entry point serialises a variable, and a user driving it from
   AutoLISP is scripting, not hand-editing.

Option 2 is the recommendation if this is picked up: it matches an existing
pattern in the tree and does not move the tables.

* Notes

Found 2026-08-14 while implementing the self-documenting conf files
(pjb's request on =lisp-configuration=, shipped in 1.8.30). Deliberately not
folded into that change, which stops at the layer that owns the settings.

* DONE — 2026-08-19 (clautolisp 1.8.53), option 2

Option 2 as recommended: =*ALDO-CONFIGURATION-WRITER*= in
=autolisp-builtins-core= — NIL, or a function of one stream —
consulted by =SAVE-ALDO-CONFIGURATION-TO=, and set at start-up by the
clautolisp tool, the layer that sees both sides. No new dependency: the
builtins system still knows nothing of =autolisp-debug-ui=.

** The semantic gap closed rather than papered over

The issue warned that the two sides hold DIFFERENT stores, so delegating
naively would save the debugger's settings instead of the AutoLISP
variable the user just assigned. The hook therefore serialises
=(clautolisp.debug.ui:read-config-variable)= — the AUTOLISP variable,
read through the bridge that already exists (=autolisp->cl-config=) —
and not =*aldo-configuration*=. Saving from AutoLISP still saves what
AutoLISP holds; only the FORM changes.

The documented defaults come from the UI's tables, which is the only
place they exist. A user who put arbitrary keys in the variable gets
them written as live data, above the commented defaults.

** Verification (4 tests, tool suite green at 100 checks)

- the header and the commented defaults are present, with the accepted
  values shown — that last one is what makes the file editable without
  the manual open beside it, so it is asserted rather than assumed;
- a setting that WAS set is written as live data and not as one more
  comment (the documentation must not cost the data);
- the file reads back as exactly the configuration it was saved from —
  one entry, not fifteen: the documented defaults stay comments and do
  not become explicit settings, which is what keeps aldo.conf a diff
  against the defaults;
- with the hook unset — a build without the debugger UI — the bare form
  is still written and still reads back. The fallback is a fallback, not
  a failure.
